  "body": {
    "id": "thm:signed-identification",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "If \\(\\mathcal M\\subseteq\\mathcal E_3\\), then for every \\(t\\in\\mathcal B\\), \\(p_t=p^\\star\\), \\(m_t=p^\\star\\mu_t^\\star\\), and \\(\\mu_t^\\star=m_t/p_t\\). Therefore every nonempty perfect-complier factorial effect is identified by \\(\\theta_S=2^{-2}\\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal B}\\chi_S(t)m_t/p_t\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\mathcal M",
      "\\mathcal E_3",
      "t",
      "\\mathcal B",
      "p_t",
      "p^\\star",
      "m_t",
      "\\mu_t^\\star",
      "S",
      "\\theta_S",
      "\\chi_S(t)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:full-support-randomization",
      "ass:instrument-independence",
      "ass:receipt-consistency",
      "ass:outcome-exclusion",
      "ass:first-moments",
      "ass:positive-perfect-share",
      "ass:support-in-model",
      "def:mobius-signature",
      "def:null-envelope",
      "def:signed-transforms",
      "def:walsh-effects",
      "thm:classification"
    ],
    "justification": "The formulas translate the behavioral envelope into estimands computable from randomized assignment cells.",
    "gap": "The signed ratios coincide algebraically with existing perfect-complier formulas under separable receipt; the new claim is their validity on the strictly larger classified envelope.",
    "consumer": "The result directly supplies all seven main and interaction effects required by a three-factor perfect-complier analysis.",
    "proof_tex": "Full assignment support makes every conditional cell functional below observable.  By receipt consistency and instrument independence, for every \\(z,t\\in\\mathcal B\\),\n\\[\nq_{zt}\n=P(G(z)=t\\mid Z=z)\n=P(G(z)=t). \\tag{15}\n\\]\nOn \\(\\{G(z)=t\\}\\), receipt consistency gives \\(D=t\\), and outcome exclusion gives \\(Y=Y(t)\\).  Therefore, using integrability and then instrument independence,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{zt}\n&=\\mathbb E_P\\!\\left[Y\\mathbf 1\\{D=t\\}\\mid Z=z\\right]\\\\\n&=\\mathbb E_P\\!\\left[Y(t)\\mathbf 1\\{G(z)=t\\}\\mid Z=z\\right]\\\\\n&=\\mathbb E_P\\!\\left[Y(t)\\mathbf 1\\{G(z)=t\\}\\right].\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{16}\n\\]\nThe support assumption and \\(\\mathcal M\\subseteq\\mathcal E_3\\) imply \\(G\\in\\mathcal E_3\\) almost surely.  For every \\(g\\in\\mathcal E_3\\), the null-envelope definition gives\n\\[\n\\sigma_t(g)=\\mathbf 1\\{g=g^\\star\\}. \\tag{17}\n\\]\nIndeed null maps have zero signature, while \\(g^\\star(z)=z\\) makes the sole nonzero summand in \\(\\sigma_t(g^\\star)\\) the assignment \\(z=t\\), with coefficient one.  Taking the finite signed sums in (15) and (16), and using (17), yields\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_t\n&=\\sum_z a_t(z)\\,\\mathbb E_P[\\mathbf 1\\{G(z)=t\\}]\n=\\mathbb E_P[\\sigma_t(G)]\n=P(G=g^\\star)=p^\\star,\\\\\nm_t\n&=\\sum_z a_t(z)\\,\\mathbb E_P[Y(t)\\mathbf 1\\{G(z)=t\\}]\n=\\mathbb E_P[Y(t)\\sigma_t(G)]\\\\\n&=\\mathbb E_P[Y(t)\\mathbf 1\\{G=g^\\star\\}]\n=p^\\star\\mu_t^\\star.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{18}\n\\]\nThe positive-share assumption licenses division by \\(p_t=p^\\star>0\\), so \\(\\mu_t^\\star=m_t/p_t\\).  Substitution into the Walsh definition gives, for every nonempty \\(S\\subseteq U\\),\n\\[\n\\theta_S=2^{-2}\\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal B}\\chi_S(t)\\frac{m_t}{p_t}.\n\\]\nThus the causal means and effects are proved equal to, rather than defined as, the displayed observable functionals.",
